__d(function(e,t,i,a,o,n,r){o.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2014 Textbook for Class VII",chapter_number:2,chapter_title:"Fractions and Decimals",estimated_study_time_minutes:65,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Recall types of fractions: proper, improper, mixed fractions","Multiply a fraction by a whole number and a fraction by a fraction","Divide a whole number by a fraction and a fraction by a fraction","Understand multiplication and division of decimal numbers","Multiply and divide decimals by 10, 100, and 1000","Convert between fractions and decimals","Apply fraction and decimal operations to real-life problems","Find a fraction of a quantity and apply in everyday contexts"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Types of fractions: proper (numerator < denominator), improper (numerator \u2265 denominator), mixed numbers","Equivalent fractions, simplest form (HCF method)","Addition and subtraction of fractions with like and unlike denominators from Class VI","Place value of decimals \u2014 tenths, hundredths, thousandths"],introduction:{why_important:"Fractions and decimals are the language of precision. Whenever we measure, share, or calculate parts of a whole, we need fractions and decimals. Recipes (\xbd cup), money (\u20b912.50), measurements (2.5 km), discounts (\xbe of the price) \u2014 all use these concepts constantly.",real_life_relevance:"A recipe calls for 2\xbd cups of flour but you want to make half the recipe \u2014 you need to multiply a fraction (\xbd \xd7 2\xbd). You buy fabric at \u20b9125.50 per metre \u2014 you need decimal multiplication. A shop offers \xbe discount \u2014 fraction of a quantity. Fuel efficiency (km per litre) uses decimal division.",karnataka_context:"Karnataka's silk saree weavers measure thread in fractions of metres. Rice is sold in kg with decimal weights (2.5 kg, 0.75 kg). Petrol prices in Karnataka are given as decimals (\u20b9102.54/litre). Ragi production statistics use decimal tonnes. Students dividing snacks equally at school use fractions every day.",connection_to_previous_learning:"Class VI introduced fractions and their addition/subtraction. Class VII extends to multiplication and division of fractions and decimals \u2014 the complete set of all four operations."},story_summary:{characters:[{name:"Proper/Improper/Mixed Fractions",role:"The three forms of fractions \u2014 understanding how to convert between them is essential for all operations"},{name:"Reciprocal",role:"The key to fraction division \u2014 dividing by a fraction means multiplying by its reciprocal"},{name:"Decimal Place Value",role:"Understanding tenths, hundredths, thousandths makes multiplication and division by powers of 10 intuitive"}],plot:["The chapter opens by reviewing fraction types: proper (3/5), improper (7/4), and mixed (1\xbe).","It develops multiplication of a fraction by a whole number: (2/3) \xd7 6 = 12/3 = 4.","It extends to fraction \xd7 fraction: (2/3) \xd7 (3/4) = 6/12 = 1/2 \u2014 multiply numerators, multiply denominators.","It introduces the reciprocal of a fraction (flip numerator and denominator) as the key tool for division.","It develops division: a \xf7 (b/c) = a \xd7 (c/b) \u2014 dividing by a fraction = multiplying by its reciprocal.","It transitions to decimals \u2014 multiplication: shifting the decimal point; 0.3 \xd7 0.4 = 0.12.","It covers multiplication and division of decimals by 10, 100, 1000 \u2014 shifting the decimal point right (\xd7) or left (\xf7).","The chapter ends with word problems integrating fraction and decimal operations."],moral:"Fractions and decimals are two sides of the same coin \u2014 every fraction can be written as a decimal and vice versa. Mastering the four operations on both gives you a complete toolkit for mathematical precision."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"Multiplication of Fractions",simple_explanation:"To multiply two fractions: multiply numerators together and multiply denominators together. Simplify if possible. To multiply a fraction by a whole number: multiply the numerator by the whole number, keep the denominator.",detailed_explanation:"Fraction \xd7 Whole Number: (a/b) \xd7 n = (a \xd7 n)/b. Example: (2/5) \xd7 3 = 6/5 = 1\u2155. Fraction \xd7 Fraction: (a/b) \xd7 (c/d) = (a\xd7c)/(b\xd7d). Example: (3/4) \xd7 (2/5) = 6/20 = 3/10. Mixed number: Convert to improper fraction first, then multiply. Example: 1\xbd \xd7 2\u2153 = (3/2) \xd7 (7/3) = 21/6 = 7/2 = 3\xbd. 'Of' means multiply: \xbe of 24 = \xbe \xd7 24 = 18. Tip: Cancel common factors before multiplying to keep numbers small.",real_life_example:"A recipe needs \xbe cup of sugar per batch. For 4 batches: \xbe \xd7 4 = 3 cups. Or: a plot is 2\xbd km long and \u2154 km wide. Area = 2\xbd \xd7 \u2154 = 5/2 \xd7 2/3 = 10/6 = 5/3 = 1\u2154 sq km.",karnataka_example:"A Karnataka silk weaver uses \u215d metre of thread per cm of pattern. For a 40 cm pattern: \u215d \xd7 40 = 25 metres of thread. Or: ragi mudde uses \xbe cup ragi flour per person. For 8 people: \xbe \xd7 8 = 6 cups.",common_mistakes:"Students forget to convert mixed numbers to improper fractions before multiplying. 2\xbd \xd7 3 \u2260 2\xd73 + \xbd\xd73 (that actually works but is inefficient \u2014 better to convert: 5/2 \xd7 3 = 15/2 = 7\xbd). Also, 'of' must be recognised as multiplication \u2014 '\xbd of 10' = \xbd \xd7 10 = 5.",memory_tip:"Multiply fractions: TOP \xd7 TOP over BOTTOM \xd7 BOTTOM. (a/b) \xd7 (c/d) = ac/bd. Cancel before multiplying if possible \u2014 it's much easier. 'Of' = multiply."},{concept_name:"Division of Fractions",simple_explanation:"To divide by a fraction, multiply by its reciprocal (flip the fraction). The reciprocal of a/b is b/a. So a \xf7 (b/c) = a \xd7 (c/b).",detailed_explanation:"Reciprocal: flip the numerator and denominator. Reciprocal of 3/4 = 4/3. Reciprocal of 5 = 1/5. Reciprocal of 1/7 = 7. Whole number \xf7 fraction: 3 \xf7 (\xbe) = 3 \xd7 (4/3) = 12/3 = 4. Fraction \xf7 whole number: (3/4) \xf7 3 = (3/4) \xd7 (1/3) = 3/12 = 1/4. Fraction \xf7 fraction: (2/3) \xf7 (4/5) = (2/3) \xd7 (5/4) = 10/12 = 5/6. Mixed numbers: convert to improper fractions first, then apply the rule.",real_life_example:"A 3-metre ribbon is cut into pieces of \xbe metre each. How many pieces? 3 \xf7 (\xbe) = 3 \xd7 (4/3) = 4 pieces. Or: if (\xbd) litre fills (\xbc) of a tank, how much does a full tank hold? Full tank = \xbd \xf7 \xbc = \xbd \xd7 4 = 2 litres.",karnataka_example:"A textile shop has 12\xbd metres of fabric. Each dress needs 2\xbd metres. How many dresses? 12\xbd \xf7 2\xbd = (25/2) \xf7 (5/2) = (25/2) \xd7 (2/5) = 50/10 = 5 dresses.",common_mistakes:"Students divide the wrong way \u2014 they take the reciprocal of the first fraction instead of the second. Remember: only FLIP (reciprocal) the fraction you are DIVIDING BY (the divisor, which is the second fraction). (a/b) \xf7 (c/d) = (a/b) \xd7 (d/c) \u2014 flip only c/d.",memory_tip:"Division = FLIP and MULTIPLY. Keep the first fraction, Change \xf7 to \xd7, Flip the second. KCF: Keep, Change, Flip. (a/b) \xf7 (c/d) \u2192 Keep (a/b), Change to \xd7, Flip (c/d) to (d/c). = (a\xd7d)/(b\xd7c)."},{concept_name:"Multiplication and Division of Decimals",simple_explanation:"Multiply decimals like whole numbers, then count the total decimal places in both numbers and place the decimal point in the answer. Dividing by 10, 100, 1000 shifts the decimal left; multiplying shifts it right.",detailed_explanation:"Multiplying decimals: Ignore decimal points, multiply as whole numbers, then count total decimal places (from both numbers) and insert the point. Example: 0.3 \xd7 0.4 \u2192 3 \xd7 4 = 12, total decimal places = 2, answer = 0.12. 2.5 \xd7 1.3 \u2192 25 \xd7 13 = 325, 2 decimal places \u2192 3.25. Dividing decimals: 2.4 \xf7 0.6 \u2192 multiply both by 10: 24 \xf7 6 = 4. Powers of 10: Multiply by 10 \u2192 shift decimal point 1 place RIGHT. Multiply by 100 \u2192 shift 2 places right. Divide by 10 \u2192 shift 1 place LEFT. Divide by 100 \u2192 shift 2 places left. Example: 2.347 \xd7 100 = 234.7. 56.3 \xf7 1000 = 0.0563.",real_life_example:"Petrol costs \u20b9102.40 per litre. You buy 12.5 litres: cost = 102.40 \xd7 12.5. Treat as 10240 \xd7 125 = 1,280,000. Count 3 decimal places \u2192 \u20b91,280.00. Or: 1 kg of rice costs \u20b948.50. Weight = 2.5 kg: cost = 48.50 \xd7 2.5 = \u20b9121.25.",karnataka_example:"Mangaluru's fish market: pomfret costs \u20b9320.50 per kg. A restaurant buys 5.5 kg: 320.50 \xd7 5.5 = 32050 \xd7 55 \xf7 1000 = 1762750 \xf7 1000 = \u20b91,762.75. Bengaluru auto fare: \u20b915.00 for first km, \u20b911.50 per km after. For 3.4 km after first: 11.50 \xd7 3.4 = \u20b939.10.",common_mistakes:"The most common error: misplacing the decimal point. Students count decimal places incorrectly. Method: count ALL decimal places in BOTH numbers and that is the total in the answer. Also: 0.1 \xd7 0.1 = 0.01 (not 0.1) \u2014 two decimal places in the factors means two in the product.",memory_tip:"Decimal multiplication: IGNORE the point, MULTIPLY the digits, COUNT the places, INSERT the point. For \xd710/100/1000: shift the decimal RIGHT (multiplication = more). For \xf710/100/1000: shift LEFT (division = less). RIGHT = more = multiplication. LEFT = less = division."}],key_terms_glossary:[{term:"Proper Fraction",meaning:"A fraction where the numerator is less than the denominator. Value is always between 0 and 1. Examples: 1/2, 3/5, 7/9."},{term:"Improper Fraction",meaning:"A fraction where the numerator is greater than or equal to the denominator. Value is \u2265 1. Examples: 7/4, 5/3, 9/9."},{term:"Mixed Number (Mixed Fraction)",meaning:"A number consisting of a whole number and a proper fraction. Examples: 2\xbd, 3\xbe. Convert to improper fraction: multiply whole number by denominator, add numerator, keep denominator."},{term:"Reciprocal",meaning:"The reciprocal of a fraction a/b is b/a (flip numerator and denominator). The product of a number and its reciprocal is always 1: (a/b) \xd7 (b/a) = 1. Reciprocal of 0 does not exist."},{term:"Product",meaning:"The result of multiplication. The product of two fractions = (product of numerators)/(product of denominators)."},{term:"Decimal",meaning:"A number expressed in the base-10 system with a decimal point separating the whole part from the fractional part. Examples: 3.14, 0.5, 12.75."},{term:"Place Value (Decimal)",meaning:"The value of each digit based on its position relative to the decimal point. Tenths (1/10), Hundredths (1/100), Thousandths (1/1000), etc."},{term:"Denominator",meaning:"The bottom number of a fraction \u2014 it tells us how many equal parts the whole is divided into."},{term:"Numerator",meaning:"The top number of a fraction \u2014 it tells us how many of the equal parts we have."},{term:"Equivalent Fractions",meaning:"Fractions that represent the same value despite having different numerators and denominators. Example: 1/2 = 2/4 = 4/8."}],worked_examples:[{example_number:1,type:"Computation",question:"Find: 2\u2153 \xd7 1\xbe",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Convert mixed numbers to improper fractions.","2\u2153 = (2\xd73+1)/3 = 7/3.","1\xbe = (1\xd74+3)/4 = 7/4.","Step 2: Multiply: (7/3) \xd7 (7/4) = 49/12.","Step 3: Convert back to mixed number: 49 \xf7 12 = 4 remainder 1 \u2192 4 1/12.","Answer: 4 1/12."]},{example_number:2,type:"Computation",question:"Find: 3\xbd \xf7 1\xbc",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Convert to improper fractions. 3\xbd = 7/2. 1\xbc = 5/4.","Step 2: Apply KCF \u2014 Keep (7/2), Change to \xd7, Flip (5/4 \u2192 4/5).","(7/2) \xd7 (4/5) = 28/10 = 14/5.","Step 3: Convert: 14 \xf7 5 = 2 remainder 4 \u2192 2\u2158.","Answer: 2\u2158."]},{example_number:3,type:"Computation",question:"Find: 1.2 \xd7 3.5",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Ignore decimal points. 12 \xd7 35 = 420.","Step 2: Count decimal places: 1.2 has 1 decimal place; 3.5 has 1 decimal place. Total = 2.","Step 3: Insert 2 decimal places in 420 \u2192 4.20 = 4.2.","Answer: 4.2."]},{example_number:4,type:"Word Problem",question:"A car travels 16.5 km per litre of fuel. How far will it travel on 8.4 litres?",step_by_step_answer:["Distance = speed \xd7 fuel = 16.5 \xd7 8.4.","Step 1: 165 \xd7 84 = 13,860.","Step 2: Total decimal places = 1 + 1 = 2.","Step 3: 13,860 \u2192 138.60 km.","Answer: The car travels 138.6 km on 8.4 litres."]}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"Fractions and Decimals \u2014 All Operations",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"What is the reciprocal of 5/7?",a:"7/5. Flip numerator and denominator."},{type:"Computation",q:"Find: (3/4) \xf7 (9/16)",a:"(3/4) \xd7 (16/9) = 48/36 = 4/3 = 1\u2153."},{type:"Computation",q:"Find: 0.07 \xd7 100",a:"Shift decimal 2 places right: 0.07 \xd7 100 = 7."},{type:"Understanding",q:"How many decimal places does the product of 1.23 \xd7 0.4 have?",a:"1.23 has 2 decimal places; 0.4 has 1. Total = 3. So product has 3 decimal places."},{type:"Application",q:"\u2157 of a class of 40 students play cricket. How many students play cricket?",a:"\u2157 \xd7 40 = 24 students."}]}],key_takeaways:["Multiply fractions: (a/b) \xd7 (c/d) = ac/bd. Simplify before or after multiplying.","Convert mixed numbers to improper fractions before multiplying or dividing.","Reciprocal of a/b = b/a. Product of a number and its reciprocal = 1.","Divide fractions: Keep the first, Change to \xd7, Flip the second (KCF). (a/b) \xf7 (c/d) = (a/b) \xd7 (d/c).","'Of' in maths means multiplication: \xbe of 20 = \xbe \xd7 20 = 15.","Multiply decimals: ignore points, multiply digits, count total decimal places, insert point.","Multiply by 10/100/1000: shift decimal RIGHT by 1/2/3 places.","Divide by 10/100/1000: shift decimal LEFT by 1/2/3 places.","To divide by a decimal: multiply both numbers by a power of 10 to eliminate the decimal in the divisor.","Fraction \u2194 Decimal: divide numerator by denominator to convert fraction to decimal."],mind_map:{root:"Fractions and Decimals",branches:[{branch:"Types of Fractions",sub:["Proper: numerator < denominator","Improper: numerator \u2265 denominator","Mixed: whole + proper fraction","Convert mixed \u2194 improper before operations"]},{branch:"Multiplication of Fractions",sub:["(a/b) \xd7 (c/d) = ac/bd","Whole number \xd7 fraction: multiply numerator","Mixed number: convert first","'Of' = multiply"]},{branch:"Division of Fractions",sub:["KCF: Keep, Change, Flip","(a/b) \xf7 (c/d) = (a/b) \xd7 (d/c)","Reciprocal = flip the fraction","Mixed: convert to improper first"]},{branch:"Decimal Multiplication",sub:["Ignore point, multiply digits","Count total decimal places","Insert decimal in product","\xd7 10/100/1000 \u2192 shift right"]},{branch:"Decimal Division",sub:["\xf7 10/100/1000 \u2192 shift left","Remove decimal from divisor: multiply both","Convert to fraction if needed"]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 2 \u2014 Fractions and Decimals. Fractions: Proper (n<d), Improper (n\u2265d), Mixed. Convert mixed to improper before operations. Fraction multiplication: (a/b)\xd7(c/d) = ac/bd; 'of' = \xd7. Fraction division: KCF \u2014 Keep first, Change \xf7 to \xd7, Flip second. Reciprocal of a/b = b/a; a \xd7 (b/a) = 1. Decimal multiplication: ignore point, multiply digits, count total decimal places, place point. \xd710 = shift decimal right 1; \xd7100 = right 2; \xd71000 = right 3. \xf710 = shift left 1; \xf7100 = left 2. Divide decimals: multiply both numbers by same power of 10 to make divisor a whole number. Word problems: identify whether to multiply or divide; convert units if needed.",must_remember_facts:["Mixed to improper: (whole \xd7 denominator + numerator)/denominator.","(a/b) \xd7 (c/d) = ac/bd \u2014 multiply tops, multiply bottoms.","KCF for division: Keep, Change (\xf7\u2192\xd7), Flip (reciprocal of divisor).","Reciprocal of a/b = b/a. Reciprocal of whole number n = 1/n.","'Of' means multiply: \xbe of 40 = \xbe \xd7 40 = 30.","Decimal \xd7 10 \u2192 move point 1 right. \xd7 100 \u2192 2 right. \xd7 1000 \u2192 3 right.","Decimal \xf7 10 \u2192 move point 1 left. \xf7 100 \u2192 2 left.","Multiply decimals: count total decimal places in both factors for answer.","Divide decimals: make divisor a whole number by multiplying both by 10/100/1000.","Fraction to decimal: divide numerator by denominator."]}},2586,[]);